{{define "pagename"}}settings{{end}}
{{define "title"}}Settings{{end}}
{{define "body"}}
{{- /* The two sentences at the top are mutually exclusive by construction: Problem is set only on
       a refused submit and Message only after one was accepted. They are rendered in the same slot
       so the operator reads the answer where they acted, and a page that showed both would be
       refusing and accepting the same submit. */ -}}
{{- if .Problem}}
<p class="problem" role="alert">{{.Problem}}</p>
{{- end}}
{{- if .Message}}
<p class="flag" role="status">{{.Message}}</p>
{{- end}}

{{- /* The banner states the machine's position, not this form's. It is the same sentence the
       status page prints for the same state, and it has to stay in agreement with it: an operator
       who switched the control and then reads a different word on the other page cannot tell which
       page is lying. */ -}}
<p class="banner">
  {{- if .Enforcement}}
    Enforcement is <strong>on</strong>: an account inside a lockout window is locked out, and the
    daemon asks again on every tick until the window closes.
  {{- else}}
    <span class="problem">Enforcement is switched off, so nothing is being locked out and nothing
    will be, whatever the schedule says.</span> Profiles, rules and assignments are all still saved
    here; they simply lock nobody until enforcement is switched back on.
  {{- end}}
</p>

<form method="post" action="/settings" class="edit-form">
  <input type="hidden" name="csrf" value="{{.CSRF}}">

  <fieldset>
    <legend>Enforcement</legend>
    {{- /* A radio pair rather than a checkbox, and the reason is which way a missing field reads.
           Every other checkbox in this UI is written so that absence is the safe direction -- a
           rule form that lost its allDay box enforces a timed window instead of an all-day one, and
           an assignment row that lost its box stops managing an account rather than starting to.
           This control is the reverse: a checkbox that sends nothing when clear would mean "stop
           locking children out" the moment a body was truncated or a control dropped, which is the
           one reading this project refuses to make silently (#873). Two named positions, and a
           request carrying neither is refused. */ -}}
    <p>
      <label><input type="radio" name="enforcement" value="on" required
                    {{if .Enforcement}}checked{{end}}>
        On &mdash; enforce the schedule</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="enforcement" value="off" required
                    {{if not .Enforcement}}checked{{end}}>
        Off &mdash; stop locking everyone out</label>
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Switching it off does not delete any profile, rule or assignment, and it does not release a
      screen that is already locked &mdash; the daemon stops asking, and whatever the desktop locked
      stays locked until it is unlocked at the machine. Turning it back on takes effect on the next
      tick without restarting the daemon.
    </p>
  </fieldset>

  <fieldset>
    <legend>How often the daemon checks</legend>
    <p>
      <label for="poll-seconds">Seconds between checks</label>
      <input id="poll-seconds" type="number" name="pollSeconds"
             value="{{.PollField}}" min="{{.MinPoll}}" max="{{.MaxPoll}}" step="1" required>
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Between {{.MinPoll}} and {{.MaxPoll}} seconds. A shorter interval locks a screen out sooner
      after a window opens and wakes the machine more often; a longer one is quieter and delays both
      the lock and the release by up to that much. A value outside the range is refused here rather
      than corrected &mdash; in particular, zero is not quietly turned into the default of its own
      accord, because a control that silently rewrites what you typed is a control you cannot trust
      to have done anything at all.
    </p>
  </fieldset>

  <p>
    <button type="submit">Save settings</button>
  </p>
</form>

<p class="muted">
  The listening address, the TLS mode and the lock backends are not on this page. They change how
  the daemon is reached and what it asks the desktop to do, so each needs its own way of saying
  "you have just cut yourself off" before it is safe to offer as a form.
</p>

<p><a href="/">Back to the status page</a></p>
{{end}}
